   CC lliibbrraarryy//kkeerrnneell ddiiffffeerreenncceess
       In glibc 2.15 and earlier, when the wrapper for this system call deter‐
       mines  that  the argument can’t be passed to the kernel without integer
       truncation (because the kernel is old and does not support 32‐bit group
       IDs), it will return -1 and set _e_r_r_n_o to EEIINNVVAALL without attempting  the
       system call.

NNOOTTEESS
       The filesystem group ID concept and the sseettffssggiidd() system call were in‐
       vented  for  historical reasons that are no longer applicable on modern
       Linux kernels.  See sseettffssuuiidd(2) for a discussion of why the use of both
       sseettffssuuiidd(2) and sseettffssggiidd() is nowadays unneeded.

       The original Linux sseettffssggiidd() system call supported only  16‐bit  group
       IDs.  Subsequently, Linux 2.4 added sseettffssggiidd3322() supporting 32‐bit IDs.
       The  glibc  sseettffssggiidd()  wrapper  function  transparently deals with the
       variation across kernel versions.

BBUUGGSS
       No error indications of any kind are returned to the  caller,  and  the
       fact  that both successful and unsuccessful calls return the same value
       makes it impossible to directly determine whether the call succeeded or
       failed.  Instead, the caller must resort to looking at the return value
       from a further call such as _s_e_t_f_s_g_i_d_(_-_1_) (which will always  fail),  in
       order  to  determine  if  a  preceding  call  to sseettffssggiidd() changed the
       filesystem group ID.  At the very least, EEPPEERRMM should be returned  when
       the call fails (because the caller lacks the CCAAPP__SSEETTGGIIDD capability).

